	    // Otherwise, If the return value is false, index of x in old array is also -1
	    //  (arrList.contains only adds x, possibly at the end of the list)
	    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1 ? false : Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX, New_Ret,  
"If the return value is true, the index of 'x' in the old array is less than or equal to that in the new array. Otherwise, if the return value is false, the index of 'x' in the old array is also -1 (arrList.contains only adds 'x', possibly at the end of the list).");
        }
    }
}
```

### <a name="add-a-custom-data-and-behavior-to-a-test-method"></a>Dodawanie niestandardowych danych i zachowań do metody testu

Możesz dodać niestandardowe dane i zachowanie do metody testu, dodając atrybut `[PexCustomData]` i `[PexMethodUnderTest]`.

```csharp
[PexMethodUnderTest]
[PexCustomData("Test Iteration: {0}", new Object[] { 1 })]
public void CustomDataTest(string target)
{
    // ... Test Body ...
}
```

`PexCustomData` umożliwia podanie ciągu formatu, który zostanie wypełniony argumentami podanymi po `new Object[] {` }. W tym przykładzie "Test Iteration: 1" zostanie wyświetlony dla testu podczas uruchamiania testu. Tekst wewnątrz ciągu formatu musi zawierać jeden argument typu string, który zostanie zamieniony na niestandardowe dane testowe. Nagłówek kolumny w programie Excel lub innej metodzie eksportu będzie również miał nazwę "Niestandardowe dane testowe"

`PexMethodUnderTest` powinien być używany tylko raz w każdym testowym typie. Określa on, która metoda należy testować przy użyciu narzędzia Pex.

### <a name="add-a-description-to-the-pexclass-for-documentation-purposes"></a>Dodawanie opisu do klasy Pex do celów dokumentacji
Użyj `[PexClass(Description = "...")]` do stosowania i innych metadanych dla klasy testowej.

```csharp
[PexClass(Description = "This is a description of the Pex class for the purpose of documentation.")]
public partial class SomeTestClass
{
    [PexMethodUnderTest]
    public int SomeTestMethod([PexAssumeValid]int x, [PexAssumeValid]int y)
    {
        // ... Test Body ...
    }
}
```

### <a name="use-the-pexmethodundertest-to-specify-the-unit-tests"></a>Używanie PexMethodUnderTest do określania testów jednostkowych
Użyj dekoratora `[PexMethodUnderTest]` w celu określenia metody, która ma zostać testowana. Takie podejście jest najlepszym rozwiązaniem, ponieważ pozwala to na kontrolę nad dokładnym przeznaczeniem testów jednostkowych.

```csharp
[PexClass]
public partial class SomeTestClass
{
    [PexMethodUnderTest]
--------------------	    System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && ((Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX) || 0 <= Old_arrListIndexOfX)));

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that   @@@ If the return value is true, last index of x in old array is a part of new array in same positions
	    System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 && 0 <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX));

        }
    }
}
```

In summary:

- The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute tells Pex that our test is checking the Contains method of the ArrayList class.
- We make some assumptions that could be beneficial later such as if x is indeed in the ArrayList, the index and position should be preserved.
- We declare some initial values for observing them next.
- After adding x to arrList, we get the new values for the Count, IndexOf (position), and LastIndexOf (position). We use these values in our assertions.

Let's run the test:

- The first assert checks whether the IndexOf method's post-change position is preserved. This is true only if the IndexOf method in the original ArrayList returned a valid result and this change does not affect the position.
- The second assert validates whether the LastIndexOf method's position in the post-change ArrayList matches the pre-change position. If the LastIndexOf is valid and still points to the same location, this passes too.

#### The Power of Equivalence Partitioning ####

Pex uses a technique called equivalence partitioning to find different test cases to validate your program. It partitions inputs into sets such that they are more likely to cause the program to behave differently. In simpler words, instead of simply checking all inputs against your program, Pex only checks those inputs that fall into a specific equivalence class.

Pex partitions inputs based on partitions defined by the PexAttributes such as PexAssumeValid, PexAssumeUnderTest, etc. Let’s apply this technique to the Contains method.

By using PexAssumeValid and PexAssumeUnderTest we can improve the partitioning and focus on testing important scenarios and behaviors in a controlled manner. This makes the testing more efficient and allows us to verify the correctness of Contains more accurately. In a nutshell, the goal of good testing is to increase coverage and make sure that the minimum surface of code is tested.

Pex is very powerful when applied correctly, and it can simplify your testing process and boost efficiency significantly. However, using Pex is not a silver bullet and it requires a good understanding of your code and the properties you care about. In a real-world scenario, sometimes it may not catch bugs that it should and other times it may spend too much time on trivial cases. The effectiveness of Pex depends on the quality of your tests, how thorough they are and how well they target the most critical areas.

In conclusion, when it comes to testing and verifying the correctness of components and functions, Pex (or any other automatic testing tool) can be a great help to improve the testing strategies and coverage. However, it is important to apply these techniques with thoughtful consideration and understanding so that they really bring the most benefit to the testing process.

Source: http://rehansaeed.com/automated-testing-with-pex-framework-part-1/

