The `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true)` indicates that the contract test precondition is always true, which is always a good practice when contract testing to ensure that the tests are safe to run. Note that the test is designed to work with the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute, which causes the system to assume that each method call is under test. This allows you to get meaningful results from your code coverage measures.

The next line runs the test method.

In this instance of the contract test, the PexObserver.Assert() method is used to ensure that the 'Contains' method operates correctly. The IndexOf and LastIndexOf methods are called to verify that the returned index position of 'x' is always increasing or unchanged.

Note that PexObserver.Assert() will only display relevant information into output window if a test fails. If both old IndexOf and LastIndexOf of 'x' are -1(which means 'x' does not exist), it means the 'Contains' method returns false, thus the test does not throw any exceptions and passes.

Lastly, a simple Assert.IsTrue condition ensures that 'Contains' method always returns true since it will return false when 'x' does not exist. 

This setup is designed to validate the correctness and robustness property of a method, including its contracts (pre and postconditions). For ArrayList, Pex is used to automatically generates the unit tests for multiple possible execution paths in parallel (a feature we call "coverage guided testing"), which makes the whole process of contract and code coverage verification more efficient.

